# Aikido scanner ignore rules for qaudion-ios.
#
# Rules use gitignore-style syntax. Every rule is paired with a rationale so
# a future reviewer (or an auditor asking why an Aikido finding was silenced)
# can verify the decision without re-doing the triage.
#
# Scope:
#   * True positives are fixed in code, NOT silenced here.
#   * Ignore rules are for documented false positives, third-party vendored
#     code we don't own, or structural scanner limitations.
#
# When adding a rule: paste the Aikido issue title + sub-issue ID in the
# comment so we can cross-reference with the dashboard.
#
# Status as of 2026-08-21 (superseded, see update below):
#   This repo was NOT YET CONNECTED to the Aikido dashboard (no findings have
#   been triaged here). Placeholder file added to match the convention used
#   in bcrypto-server, qaudion-android-new, and qaudion-desktop, so the
#   moment this repo is connected in Aikido's external dashboard, the triage
#   file already exists in the expected location/format and the first real
#   findings can be added here following the numbered-section pattern used
#   in the sibling repos' .aikidoignore files.
#
# Update 2026-10: the repo is now scanned by Aikido; the first triaged false
# positives are the numbered sections below.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. PUBLIC verification keys flagged as "exposed secret" (HIGH)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `EntitlementPublicKey.swift` and `WipeSigningPublicKey.swift` (and the SPKI
# PEM build assets they load, `bcrypto_entitlement_pubkey.pem` and
# `wipe_signing_pubkey.pem`) hold ONLY the *public* half of an Ed25519
# keypair, pinned in the app to VERIFY server-signed entitlements and wipe
# commands. A public verification key is not a secret by design: shipping it
# in the binary is the whole point of pinning. The matching private keys live
# server-side and never appear in this repository (verified: no
# `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` block in any tracked source file).
#
# Matches Aikido issues: "Exposed secret" (HIGH) in the two Swift files above.
QAudionEngine/Sources/QAudionEngine/Entitlements/EntitlementPublicKey.swift
QAudionEngine/Sources/QAudionEngine/Security/WipeSigningPublicKey.swift
QAudionEngine/Sources/QAudionEngine/Resources/bcrypto_entitlement_pubkey.pem
QAudionEngine/Sources/QAudionEngine/Resources/wipe_signing_pubkey.pem

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. Synthetic JWT test vectors in the log-redaction self-tests (HIGH)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `scripts/ship-ios-logs.py` (built-in redaction self-test, ~line 3031) and
# `scripts/test_redact_structured_parity.py` (~line 96) contain a
# deliberately fake JWT whose payload decodes to `{"sub":"synthetic"}` with a
# placeholder signature ("synthetic" marker in the source comment). It is the
# NEGATIVE test input that proves the redactor masks JWT-shaped strings; it
# authenticates nothing. The same string exists in git history, which is why
# Aikido reports "JWT found in git history": no credential was ever exposed,
# so no rotation is required.
#
# Matches Aikido issue: "JWT found in git history" (HIGH).
scripts/ship-ios-logs.py
scripts/test_redact_structured_parity.py
